WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:05.560
OK, I want to pick up on this idea of the
U.S. having the lowest fatality rate when

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.410
it comes to Corona virus.

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:13.140
Donald Trump has been saying this a lot and
said it to Chris Wallace during that completely

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:16.860
Gong Show Fox News interview that we looked
at earlier.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:20.560
And there are different metrics for different
questions.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.369
And you are.

00:00:22.369 --> 00:00:26.420
I'm not saying you're being tricked, but there
are attempts being made to trick you, which

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:28.400
hopefully you aren't falling for.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.300
So let's start at the beginning.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:35.969
If we want to know where more people are dying,
you look at the number of deaths, right.

00:00:35.969 --> 00:00:40.979
The problem is that number would not be adjusted
for the population because different countries

00:00:40.979 --> 00:00:42.179
have different populations.

00:00:42.179 --> 00:00:44.280
So we want to look at per capita.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:48.399
If I say to you, a thousand people died of
the virus in the U.S. and a thousand people

00:00:48.399 --> 00:00:49.839
died in Norway.

00:00:49.839 --> 00:00:51.359
It's a thousand people in both cases.

00:00:51.359 --> 00:00:53.179
But Norway has a smaller population.

00:00:53.179 --> 00:00:54.570
So a thousand people dying.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:58.859
There is more per capita than in the United
States.

00:00:58.859 --> 00:01:00.250
That's very simple.

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:04.530
But I want to go in much more detail and look
at three different metrics and tell you where

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:06.510
they work and where they don't work.

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:08.610
We have deaths per capita.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:13.350
We have the case fatality rate and we have
the infection fatality rate.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:17.002
Yesterday, Donald Trump, in his interview
with Chris Wallace, said we have one of the

00:01:17.002 --> 00:01:19.480
lowest fatality rates in the world.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:23.040
And they put up a graph that the White House
gave Fox News.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.100
And in this one, you have the U.K. at the
top.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:26.100
You have the U.S. in the middle.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:28.390
And you have Russia sort of closer to the
bottom.

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:32.880
But if you look at the top of this page, this
is the case fatality rate.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:38.850
So the case fatality rate is simply deaths
divided by confirmed cases.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:43.750
So as you can immediately notice, the case
fatality rate depends on the number of tests

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:48.409
that you do if one person dies and one hundred
people tested positive.

00:01:48.409 --> 00:01:51.210
That is a case fatality rate of one percent.

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:52.370
One out of 100.

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:58.490
Notice that you can lower your case fatality
rate by doing more tests if one person dies.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:00.560
But a test, a hundred more people meaning?

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:03.740
Now, I've done 200 positive tests.

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:07.409
Now your case fatality rate is only zero point
five percent.

00:02:07.409 --> 00:02:11.989
Nothing has changed about the portion of the
population that has died.

00:02:11.989 --> 00:02:15.769
You've just tested more people and lowered
your case fatality rate.

00:02:15.769 --> 00:02:20.579
So as you can see, you can lower CFR by testing
more people.

00:02:20.579 --> 00:02:26.459
Interestingly, Trump's strategy has been to
test fewer people, which hurts his own strategy

00:02:26.459 --> 00:02:28.799
of focusing on case fatality rate.

00:02:28.799 --> 00:02:31.629
So that gets us to infection fatality rate.

00:02:31.629 --> 00:02:38.510
So when you look at infection, fatality, that's
deaths divided by the total believe the number

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:39.510
of infections.

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:42.629
So remember, we're not testing everybody.

00:02:42.629 --> 00:02:46.609
There are people who have had coronavirus
who never got a.

00:02:46.609 --> 00:02:51.840
So when we have 100 positive tests, we have
to estimate, well, how many people were really

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:52.840
infected?

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:56.970
We might determine that there's a 2.5 times
infection rate.

00:02:56.970 --> 00:03:05.980
So for every hundred tests that go positive,
you estimate you really have 250 believed

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:06.980
cases.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:14.700
So the infection, fatality rate with one death,
over 250 believed cases is only zero point

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:15.739
four percent.

00:03:15.739 --> 00:03:21.710
Now, what's important is you can lower your
infection fatality rate by arguing that there

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:25.430
were way more asymptomatic people that had
the virus and recovered and are fine.

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:31.279
So if I argue, you know, for every positive
test, there's probably 10 people who had it,

00:03:31.279 --> 00:03:35.709
I lower my infection fatality rate dramatically.

00:03:35.709 --> 00:03:37.510
That now gets us two deaths.

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:44.400
Per capita deaths per capita are not tied
to testing or any speculation about asymptomatic

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:45.400
spread.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:49.450
You're simply dividing deaths by population
one death in a country of 100 people.

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:52.029
That's one percent deaths per capita.

00:03:52.029 --> 00:03:58.019
Notice that deaths per capita only goes up
over time, assuming that the population isn't

00:03:58.019 --> 00:04:00.150
growing explosively during a pandemic.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:05.970
So when a second person dies in a country
of 100, your deaths per capita are now up

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:07.950
to two percent.

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:13.950
This is important because deaths per capita
won't be impacted by the testing level.

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:18.590
Deaths per capita won't be impacted by your
estimate of how many asymptomatic cases they

00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:19.590
are.

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:24.690
And when you look at that, the U.S. is currently
10th highest in the world.

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:28.140
Are deaths per million are 432.

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:32.310
We're right behind Chile, which is at 442.

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:37.000
So out of 200 plus countries and territories,
we are tenth in the world.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:41.990
If you get rid of San Marino and Andorra,
which are tiny, and when you include countries

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:45.420
so small, it can sometimes be deceptive.

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:49.910
We're really eighth in the world among reasonably
sized countries.

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:55.290
So the way that deaths per capita can be affected
is that there could be corona virus deaths

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:57.890
that aren't being counted as corona virus
deaths.

00:04:57.890 --> 00:05:01.850
So you have to look at excess deaths compared
to last year.

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:07.260
If you do that, it seems as though we are
probably undercounting the number of corona

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:08.550
virus deaths.

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:13.640
So now, you know, when Trump says we have
the lowest death rate, he's probably referring

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:18.730
to the case fatality rate, even though he's
also wrong that ours is low.

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:22.480
But really, we should focus on deaths per
capita over time.

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:26.880
If you want to compare how countries are doing,
you want to look at deaths per capita.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:33.190
If you want to look at how deadly the disease
is among people who get it, you would look

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:35.500
at CFR and IFR.

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:39.850
But for for Trump's purpose, which is compare
how we're doing to other countries deaths

00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:43.090
per capita is the metric that we should be
looking at.

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:48.130
And of course, it's never the metric that
Trump or Calima Kennedy or any of them are

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:50.530
actually talking about.

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:51.810
Hopefully this is useful.

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:56.090
And we'll have some of the example data on
the show's Instagram page.

00:05:56.090 --> 00:06:28.460
Follow us on Instagram.

00:06:28.460 --> 00:07:27.150
David, tachy text, The David Pakman Show David
Pakman dot com.

